// Generic socket-option passthrough (wave-2 fill; the Python net
// census ranked it the highest-value single gap: one pair of
// functions covers every option that has no dedicated wrapper
// above). level/optname are the raw OS numbers -- the SLA-facing
// named subset lives in sa_std/net.sla (SA_NET_SOCKOPT_LEVEL_*,
// SA_NET_SO_*, SA_NET_TCP_*, SA_NET_IP_*, SA_NET_IPV6_*). The handle
// may be any socket handle (tcp stream, tcp listener, udp socket;
// unix datagram sockets are udp-kind handles). The byte form passes
// the option value through verbatim, so struct-valued options
// (linger, mreq, timeval) are the caller's layout responsibility.
// getsockopt reports in out_len the byte count the kernel returned
// (at most out_cap); size the buffer for the option's full value.
@extern sa_std_net_socket_setsockopt(handle: u64, level: i32, optname: u32, &value: ptr, value_len: u64) -> i32
@extern sa_std_net_socket_getsockopt(handle: u64, level: i32, optname: u32, &out: ptr, out_cap: u64, &out_len: ptr) -> i32
@extern sa_std_net_socket_setsockopt_int(handle: u64, level: i32, optname: u32, value: i32) -> i32
@extern sa_std_net_socket_getsockopt_int(handle: u64, level: i32, optname: u32, &out_value: ptr) -> i32

// Datagram receive with per-packet info (wave-2 fill; the Unreal
// net census datagram trio: pktinfo + packet timestamp, RecvMulti
// below). One recvmsg reports, alongside the sender address
// (out_addr, an owned NetAddr handle like udp_recv_from's):
//   - the destination address the datagram was sent to, from the
//     IP_PKTINFO / IPV6_PKTINFO control message (out_dest_addr, an
//     owned NetAddr handle whose port is this socket's local port),
//   - the receiving interface index (out_ifindex),
//   - the kernel receive timestamp from SO_TIMESTAMP, as UTC epoch
//     nanoseconds (out_timestamp_ns).
// out_flags is a bitmask of SA_NET_RECV_MSG_* (net.sla): PKTINFO
// when a pktinfo control message arrived, TIMESTAMP when a
// timestamp control message arrived, CTRUNC / TRUNC mirroring the
// recvmsg MSG_CTRUNC / MSG_TRUNC bits. The caller enables delivery
// first (IP_PKTINFO or IPV6_RECVPKTINFO, SO_TIMESTAMP) through the
// passthrough above; without it the info outs stay zero and the
// flags stay clear -- absence is data, never an error. Any out
// pointer except out_read may be omitted. Linux only: other
// platforms return SA_STD_ERR_UNSUPPORTED.
@extern sa_std_net_udp_recv_msg(socket: u64, &buf: ptr, cap: u64, &out_read: ptr, &out_addr: ptr, &out_dest_addr: ptr, &out_ifindex: ptr, &out_timestamp_ns: ptr, &out_flags: ptr) -> i32

// Batch datagram receive (recvmmsg; the third datagram-trio item).
// The receive buffer is max_msgs fixed-size slots of slot_size
// bytes laid end to end (buf_cap must cover slot_size * max_msgs).
// The call waits for the first datagram (bounded by the socket's
// read timeout / blocking mode, MSG_WAITFORONE semantics), then
// drains every datagram already queued, up to max_msgs. out_count
// receives the number of datagrams read; out_lens (max_msgs u64
// slots, required) receives each datagram's length in order;
// out_addrs (max_msgs u64 slots, optional) receives one owned
// NetAddr sender handle per datagram. max_msgs is capped at 64.
// A datagram longer than slot_size is truncated to its slot and
// its out_lens entry reports the truncated length. Linux only:
// other platforms return SA_STD_ERR_UNSUPPORTED.
@extern sa_std_net_udp_recv_multi(socket: u64, &buf: ptr, buf_cap: u64, slot_size: u64, max_msgs: u64, &out_count: ptr, &out_lens: ptr, &out_addrs: ptr) -> i32

// Peer credentials for a connected unix datagram socket
// (SO_PEERCRED; the stream form is sa_std_net_unix_stream_peer_cred
// above -- the Java net census named this face). Meaningful for a
// connected socket (e.g. from sa_std_net_unix_datagram_pair, or
// after sa_std_net_unix_datagram_connect); for an unconnected
// datagram socket the kernel answers with the zero credential
// (pid 0), which is the no-peer sentinel, not an error.
@extern sa_std_net_unix_datagram_peer_cred(socket: u64, &out_pid: ptr, &out_uid: ptr, &out_gid: ptr) -> i32

// Raw-socket creation (wave-3 fill; the primitive the ICMP
// transport half was blocked on). domain / sock_type / protocol are
// the raw OS numbers (the sockopt passthrough convention):
// AF_INET=2 / AF_INET6=10 (SA_NET_AF_* in net.sla), SOCK_RAW=3 or
// SOCK_DGRAM=2 (the Linux ping-socket form; SOCK_STREAM and other
// types are rejected), IPPROTO_ICMP=1 / IPPROTO_ICMPV6=58. The
// socket is registered as a datagram-kind handle, so
// sa_std_net_udp_send_to / udp_recv_from / udp_set_read_timeout,
// the sockopt passthrough, sa_std_fd_into_raw and sa_std_close all
// apply to it. Creation can fail with a permission status (root /
// CAP_NET_RAW for SOCK_RAW, net.ipv4.ping_group_range for the ping
// form); that is a loud status, never a fake handle. Not
// implemented by the Windows root (ABI unsupported manifest).
@extern sa_std_net_raw_socket(domain: u32, sock_type: u32, protocol: u32, &out_handle: ptr) -> i32

// Zero-copy file-to-socket send (sendfile(2); wave-3 fill).
// Sends up to count bytes of file starting at offset over the TCP
// stream (the file's own position is never used or moved). out_sent
// receives the bytes actually sent: equal to count unless the file
// ended first (SA_STD_OK with a short count). out_next_offset may
// be omitted; when given it receives offset + out_sent. Linux only:
// other platforms return SA_STD_ERR_UNSUPPORTED, and the Windows
// root does not implement it (ABI unsupported manifest).
@extern sa_std_net_tcp_stream_send_file(stream: u64, file: u64, offset: u64, count: u64, &out_sent: ptr, &out_next_offset: ptr) -> i32

